{"real": true, "reason": "Both halves of the claim hold in the code.

1. Late joiners get no tree state. menu.gd:67-69 makes HOST/JOIN load the sandbox when `--sandbox` is passed, so a client can join the glade. grove.gd plants the trees from the seed, so a joiner sees every tree whole. ChopSystem (systems/chop_system.gd) has no peer-join hook and no rpc_id. Its only tree messages are broadcast events: _tree_struck at line 102, _tree_knock at 96 and _tree_felled at 104. SpawnSystem's per-joiner resend (spawn_system.gd:82-93) sends only actors. So a tree the server has felled stays standing on the joiner. The server's _strike then rejects hits on it (`best.felled`, line 95) and sends knock events. On the server that tree is a short stump collider (chop_tree.gd:143-145), while the client still has the full trunk. A notched tree on the joiner gets the next _tree_struck applied to a blank model at the server's cut height (line 113), so its notch depth and area differ from the server's. Because the server alone decides the fall from its own model, the joiner's fall never matches what it sees.

2. Fall physics runs on every peer. apply_fall (chop_tree.gd:136-187) builds a free RigidBody3D with a HingeJoint3D on every peer, and _physics_process (202-219) runs on all peers with no is_server check. When the top settles it is frozen and switched to collision_layer 1 (217-218) wherever it happens to rest on that peer. No transform is ever synced. Jolt is not deterministic across machines, and the event arrives at a different physics tick on clients. So the server's resting log, which blocks server-side movement, can differ from the client's.", "better_fix": "The proposed fix is right in direction but incomplete in two ways.

(a) Make the server the only owner of the top's physics. On clients, create top_body frozen (FREEZE_MODE_KINEMATIC) with no joint. Have the server stream the top's transform while it falls, unreliable at about 20 Hz, or add it to a MultiplayerSynchronizer created on apply_fall. When the server settles, send one reliable final transform; clients snap to it and switch to layer 1. Snapping only at the end, as proposed, leaves a visible jump.

(b) For late joiners, add a ChopSystem.on_peer_joined(peer) called from the server's peer_connected handler, next to the SpawnSystem resend. Send rpc_id(peer, ...) per touched tree with the model state, not a replay of strikes. Replaying strikes with energy values works only if the model is deterministic, which it is, since it is pure GDScript math. The state to send:
- cut_h and the strike list (or the notch arrays);
- felled, fall_dir and hinge;
- for a felled tree, the top's current or settled transform and settled flag.
For a settled tree, the client builds the stump and puts the frozen top directly at that transform, without a hinge or simulation."}
